    </SdkVersion>
    <Options>
      <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false" greenerHomesTab="false">
        <Vermiculite code="1">
          <English>Unknown</English>
          <French>Inconnu</French>
        </Vermiculite>
      </Main>
      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>An air-sealing grant is available if the air tightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air
change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires
that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

By air sealing and adding an HRV you could also be eligible for a $5000 Yukon Good Energy Rebate.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>By upgrading your wall insulation you could be eligible for up to $12,000 from the Yukon Good Energy Rebates.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>You are also eligible for a $2500 Good Energy insulation rebate if you increase the attic insulation to R-60 or better.</ceilings>
                <foundation>To receive the insulation rebate for below-ground walls:

    the insulation level must be R20 or less prior to the renovation; and
    you must increase it by at least R20.
Up to $10,000 for insulating below-ground walls
</foundation>
                <windows>You are also eligible for an additional $125 per window through the Good Energy
window rebate.

If you decide to replace your windows make sure to leave the manufacturers stickers on until the post assessment.
</windows>
                <Renewables>Adding a solar PV system to your home would allow for onsite self-generation, which
would reduce home electrical bills by offsetting your consumption from the grid, and by
potentially earning annual reimbursement from export to the grid via the Microgeneration Program. You would be also eligible for the Good Energy rebate of $800 per
kW of generating capacity to a maximum of $5,000 per system per year.
For every 1.0 kW DC of array generating capacity installed in this region, a Yukoner can
expect approximately 1,000 kWh of onsite generation annually. A 7.5 kW solar PV
array would generate approximately 7,500 kWh/yr and provide approximately $1,425
in annual offset savings and reimbursement earnings through the MicroGeneration
Program
</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include:
Good Energy Program website: 
https://yukon.ca/good-energy

E-rebate portal direct link
https://yukon.myenergyxpert.com/
</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
      <GreenerHomes basementSlabInsulated="false" moistureProofCrawlSpace="false" waterproofing="false" roofingMembrane="false" minR10ContinuousExposedFloors="false" backwaterValve="false" sumpPump="false" smartThermostats="false" remoteCommunities="false" evaluationCost="">
        <Community>N/A</Community>
      </GreenerHomes>
